When I travel on the Route 70 toward Glenferrie road, there's a moment that always trips me up. I cruise past Kooyongkoot Road, and in preparation for my stop I pull the string to request my stop. The 70 only runs the old style trams with the string, so I make sure to savour it before the old trams get phased out. 

But as I am mid string-pull-savour, I'm snapped back into reality. An announcement comes over the loud speaker: "Now arriving at **Berkeley Street**". I've just requested one stop early! And now I'm going to be late to get frozen yoghurt on Glenferrie Road.

Remarkably, the distance between Berkeley Street to Kooyongkoot Road is just 116 metres. This is a tiny distance, better served on foot than by tram. With both stops serving the same chunk of unassuming Inner East suburbia, this very well could be the most useless tram stop in Melbourne. Per-stop ridership data is not available, so to figure out the most useless stop let's go with a helpful proxy: **which two Melbourne tram stops are closest together?**

This is a gripping question that has been eating away at me for a few months now. But then one day, I woke up and remembered I know how to code. Thinking about the problem, it really didn't seem that hard. And it wasn't. I finished most of the work in an afternoon. 

Here's the top three:


